  ('gcid:camera_repair_shop','location','location','active',ARRAY['CLA (Clean, Lubricate, Adjust)','shutter curtain','rangefinder calibration','mirror box','aperture blades','light seals','capacitor reformation','back focus adjustment','film gate','delamination']::text[],'["CLA (Clean, Lubricate, Adjust) for film cameras", "Digital sensor cleaning and dust spot removal", "Shutter speed calibration and repair", "Lens element cleaning and fungus removal", "Light seal replacement for film cameras", "Autofocus system diagnosis and repair", "Mirror and viewfinder repair", "Flash unit capacitor replacement and repair", "Rangefinder calibration and alignment", "Water damage assessment and restoration"]'::jsonb,ARRAY['No federal licensing is required specifically for camera repair, but technicians handling refrigerant-based cooling systems in specialty cameras must hold EPA Section 608 certification.','Consumer protection laws in all US states require written estimates and customer authorization before repair costs exceed a disclosed threshold (varies by state, e.g., California''s Electronic Waste Recycling Act for disposal of electronic components).','Shops must comply with FTC regulations on advertising, including truthful claims about ''authorized service center'' status — using a brand''s logo or ''authorized'' designation without official manufacturer agreement constitutes deceptive advertising.','Electronic waste (e-waste) disposal regulations apply when discarding camera circuit boards, batteries, and CRTs; shops must use certified e-waste recyclers per state laws (e.g., California, New York, Illinois have mandatory e-waste programs).','Shops accepting used camera equipment for trade or purchase may be subject to state secondhand dealer laws requiring record-keeping and hold periods to assist law enforcement in tracking stolen goods.','If the shop sells repaired or refurbished goods, implied warranty obligations under state UCC (Uniform Commercial Code) statutes apply, and ''as-is'' disclaimers must be conspicuously disclosed in writing.','OSHA standards apply to workplace use of chemical solvents (isopropyl alcohol, lens cleaning fluids) and compressed air, requiring proper ventilation and SDS (Safety Data Sheets) on hand.','Collection and storage of customer personal data for repair tickets must comply with applicable state privacy laws such as the California Consumer Privacy Act (CCPA) if the business meets relevant thresholds.']::text[],'["Awareness: Customer notices camera malfunction, shutter failure, or image quality issues and searches online for local camera repair specialists.", "Consideration: Customer compares shops by reading reviews, checking credentials, requesting estimates, and evaluating turnaround times.", "Decision: Customer drops off equipment based on technician expertise, transparent pricing, warranty offered, and trust signals like brand authorizations.", "Retention: Customer returns for future repairs, sensor cleanings, or maintenance and refers friends after a satisfactory repair experience."]'::jsonb,'["Accurate and complete Google Business Profile with correct primary category (Camera Repair Shop) and relevant secondary categories such as Electronics Repair Shop", "Volume and recency of Google reviews mentioning specific services (e.g., ''sensor cleaning,'' ''Nikon repair,'' ''film camera CLA'') to reinforce keyword relevance", "Proximity of the physical shop location to the searcher or the city center for queries like ''camera repair near me''", "Consistent NAP (Name, Address, Phone) citations across key directories including Yelp, Yellow Pages, and photography-specific platforms", "On-website local SEO signals including city/neighborhood name in title tags, headers, and service pages targeting specific camera brands and repair types", "Google Business Profile attributes such as appointment booking link, hours of operation accuracy, uploaded photos of the shop and technicians, and active Q&A responses"]'::jsonb,ARRAY['brought my old film camera back to life','sensor cleaning made a huge difference','fair estimate before any work was done','quick turnaround, ready in a few days','really knowledgeable about vintage cameras','shutter was sticking and now it works perfectly','honest about what was and wasn''t worth fixing','handled my lens with care','reasonably priced compared to sending it to the manufacturer','the only shop I trust with my gear']::text[],ARRAY['How to Know When Your Camera Needs a Professional CLA vs. DIY Cleaning','Film Camera Light Seal Replacement: What to Expect and Why It Matters','Digital Sensor Cleaning: How Often Should You Have It Done?','Understanding Shutter Curtain Repair Costs for Popular Camera Brands','Lens Fungus: Causes, Prevention, and Professional Removal Options','How to Choose a Reputable Camera Repair Shop in Your City','Vintage Camera Restoration: Breathing New Life Into Classic Film Equipment']::text[],'["Manufacturer-authorized service center designation (e.g., Nikon, Canon, Sony Authorized Repair)", "Membership in the Photography and Imaging Professionals association or similar trade organizations", "Technician certifications or formal training from camera manufacturers'' service training programs", "Years of documented experience with specific brands and camera formats (35mm, medium format, digital mirrorless)", "Visible portfolio of before/after repairs and customer testimonials on the website", "Warranty offered on completed repairs (e.g., 90-day parts and labor guarantee) demonstrating confidence in workmanship"]'::jsonb,'{"profile_completeness": 0.2, "review_volume": 0.2, "listing_coverage": 0.15, "content_freshness": 0.15, "schema_completeness": 0.15, "ai_visibility": 0.15}'::jsonb,100,false),
  ('gcid:camping_farm','location','location','active',ARRAY['glamping','agritourism','primitive camping','hook-up site (electric/water/sewer)','dry camping','pull-through site','back-in site','farm stay','working farm experience','seasonal pitch']::text[],'["Tent camping sites with or without electric hookups", "RV and camper van sites with full or partial hookups", "Glamping accommodations (yurts, bell tents, cabins, shepherds huts)", "Farm stay lodging in on-site farmhouses or cottages", "Guided farm tours and animal feeding experiences", "U-pick produce activities (berries, vegetables, pumpkins)", "Campfire areas and communal outdoor cooking facilities", "Equestrian camping and horse boarding for guests", "Event hosting (birthday parties, corporate retreats, school field trips)", "On-site farm market selling local produce, eggs, and honey"]'::jsonb,ARRAY['State agritourism liability protection laws (enacted in most US states) require farms to post a specified warning notice to limit landowner liability for recreational injuries; exact language and signage requirements vary by state.','Zoning and land-use permits: Operating a campground on agricultural land typically requires a conditional use permit or special-use permit from the county, and local zoning ordinances may restrict the number of sites or structures.','Health department regulations: Any campground offering sanitation facilities (restrooms, showers, dump stations) must obtain a campground operating license from the state or county health department and meet minimum sanitation standards (40 CFR and state equivalents).','FTC Endorsement Guidelines (16 CFR Part 255): Soliciting or incentivizing online reviews requires clear disclosure; farms cannot post fake reviews or offer compensation without disclosure.','ADA Accessibility (Americans with Disabilities Act): Campgrounds open to the public must provide a minimum number of accessible campsites, restrooms, and pathways per DOJ Title III and ADA Standards for Accessible Design.','Food handling and cottage food laws: Selling farm-produced food (jams, baked goods, produce) from on-site stands may require a cottage food permit, farm market license, or food handler certification depending on the state.','Fire safety and open-burn regulations: Campfire and bonfire areas must comply with local fire codes, and burn bans issued by state forestry agencies must be enforced; adequate fire suppression equipment must be on-site.','Sales tax collection: Most states require collection of sales tax on campsite rentals and retail farm-market sales; some states exempt agricultural produce sales but tax lodging and campsite fees.']::text[],'["Awareness: Traveler discovers the camping farm via Google search, social media, or agritourism directory while planning a rural getaway.", "Consideration: Customer reviews site photos, reads guest reviews, checks amenity lists and pricing, and compares available dates on a booking platform.", "Booking: Guest reserves a specific site or accommodation type directly through the farm''s website, phone, or third-party booking tool and pays a deposit.", "Post-Stay: Guest shares photos on social media, leaves a Google or TripAdvisor review, and signs up for the farm''s newsletter for return-visit promotions."]'::jsonb,'["Google Business Profile category accuracy: Primary category set to ''Camping farm'' with relevant secondary categories (Campground, Farm, Bed & breakfast) to maximize visibility across related queries.", "Review quantity and recency: A steady stream of recent 4- and 5-star reviews on the GBP listing signals activity and trustworthiness to Google''s local ranking algorithm.", "NAP consistency: Exact match of business Name, Address, and Phone number across the GBP listing, website, and all agritourism and campground directories.", "Proximity and service-area relevance: Physical farm location relative to searcher, combined with GBP service-area settings, influences ranking for ''camping nea